{% sec 1, "Eight Firms, One Industry", "Who are the big names, and how should I read them?", "Eight firms" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Think of these eight firms like the big teams in a sport. They all play the same game, but each has its own history, home ground and style: one is famous for its defence, another for speed, another for a youth academy. Knowing who's who, and why, is part of speaking the language of the industry, and interviewers notice when you can tell them apart.</p>
    <p>Four things to keep in mind as you read:</p>
  </div>
  <ul class="facts">
    <li><span class="n">1</span><span>They're all private<span class="why">None is listed on a stock exchange, so revenue figures mostly come from press reports citing people familiar with the results, or from accounts filed by their UK and Dutch companies. Treat them as good estimates, not audited totals.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">2</span><span>They trade their own money<span class="why">All eight are market makers or proprietary trading firms (often both), not hedge funds. Market Makers, Prop Firms and Hedge Funds explains the difference.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">3</span><span>2025 was a record year for most<span class="why">Volatile markets mean more trading and wider spreads, which suits market makers. Their results swing with the market, so one year doesn't define a firm.</span></span></li>
    <li><span class="n">4</span><span>Figures are as reported by November 2026<span class="why">Currencies are converted to dollars at rough 2025 averages where needed.</span></span></li>
  </ul>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Firm</th><th>Founded</th><th>Home</th><th>Best known for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Jane Street</td><td>2000</td><td>New York</td><td>ETFs, bonds, options; the industry's largest by revenue</td></tr>
        <tr><td>XTX Markets</td><td>2015</td><td>London</td><td>Machine learning; one of the biggest liquidity providers in FX</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Citadel Securities</td><td>2002</td><td>Miami</td><td>US shares and options, especially retail orders; Treasuries</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Optiver</td><td>1986</td><td>Amsterdam</td><td>Options market making worldwide</td></tr>
        <tr><td>IMC</td><td>1989</td><td>Amsterdam</td><td>Options and ETF market making</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hudson River Trading</td><td>2002</td><td>New York</td><td>Engineering-led, high-speed trading across many markets</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Jump Trading</td><td>1999</td><td>Chicago</td><td>High-speed futures trading; crypto</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Susquehanna (SIG)</td><td>1987</td><td>Philadelphia area</td><td>Options, decision-making and poker culture</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

