{% sec 3, "How Big Are They?", "How do these firms compare with each other, and with banks?", "How big are they?" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The headline is how far the biggest firms have outgrown the banks' trading desks. Reuters reported that Jane Street's 2025 net trading revenue of about \(\$39.6\)bn was more than JPMorgan's markets business, with around \(3{,}500\) employees against a bank of hundreds of thousands. And 2026 has been bigger still: Citadel Securities reportedly made about \(\$7.3\)bn in the second quarter alone, and Hudson River Trading about \(\$11.4\)bn.</p>
    <p>Revenue per employee shows the other story: these are tiny organisations for the money they handle. A firm that grosses billions with a few hundred or a few thousand people is mostly technology and capital, which is the scale moat from Competition, Speed and Edge.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Revenue and people</h3><p>Reported 2025 figures. Switch between total revenue and revenue per employee (shown only where both revenue and headcount have been reported). Jump and Susquehanna don't appear because their results aren't public.</p></div>
